ll  um i agree like i would second talk of  course  um and then others would be throughout  the journey in the arbuckle leadership  program that i think  jonathan maybe had mentioned as well um  and just overall echoing the talk piece  i think stanford is just like very good  at dealing with like  emotions uh in a way they asked me to  participate in a  role play in front of classmates  that were being trained to be coaches  basically  and during that role play i was a  coachee and they started nudging me into  like  topics of identity and race and a ton of  like things that ended up like crying in  front of everybody and the fact that  it was fine like it was it was not a  surprise it was almost like and  whatever i mentioned of like the room  kind of like holds you  in a way i think that was like my my  moment of  oh like it i i kind of fit here like  this is  this is a a good place to be right now  we um we tragically lost three  classmates from my class at stanford  and um i i hesitate to share that  because it's a really sad moment but  honestly like the way the community came  around  and and just it just instantly bonded us  like it happened in the first few weeks  of school and so  um that just sticks out to me because  there was that moment where we all  looked each other and we're like  we're gonna help each other get through  this incredibly difficult time and from  people in my class and also the way the  school handled it it was like an  immediate moment of like  wow i'm in the exact right place um so  grateful to this day for how the school  handled it and the people around me  a lot of very moving examples  um well i want to be cognizant of the  time i know we are coming up towards the  um  end of our session today  i want to um thank our panelists for  sharing your perspectives today  and for all of our attendees i want to  thank you for your attention  as well and your interest in stanford we  hope that this is just the start of your  stanford gsb j